One of the things Hank Paulson insisted on today during his Capitol Hill grilling was that there was no legitimate Bank of America could have abandoned the deal to buy Merrill Lynch by invoking the ��material adverse change�� clause of the acquisition contract. The clause was written in a way that pretty much locks out any argument Bank of America could have made.
The MAC clause, which was made public when the agreement was attached as an exhibit to the proxy asking Bank of America shareholders to approve the deal, allowed Bank of America to pull out of the deal only in very limited circumstances. It specifically says that changes to general market conditions or price levels are not reasons that can be used to abandon the deal.
In short, the game was up when the initial contract was signed. Bank of America CEO Ken Lewis�� protests in December were too little, too late. What��s more, he probably knew this at the time. Certainly, his lawyers would have told him as much. He was bluffing when he threatened to call the MAC. This means that the Hank Paulson��s insistence that he go through with the deal probably should not be viewed as government meddling so much as the government just refusing to give Lewis special permission back out of the deal.
The only way we can see for the MAC to have applied would be if Bank of America discovered that some huge bundle of assets had been incorrectly marked as being more valuable than they were. Just ordinary changes in correctly applied asset marks—that is, write-downs—would not do the trick. Basically, Lewis would have to allege that fraud or serious error had occurred in the earlier marks.
But it��s been months since this controversy came to life. Lewis has had plenty of opportunity to present evidence of fraud. He hasn��t. It��s safe to assume that he can��t or won��t. In which case, the MAC clause issue should simply be put to rest.
