By this time, festgoers may think they have seen the last of summer carnivals, eat-a-thons and craft fairs. But don't worry, fun seekers. Schaumburg has saved one of the best for last.
Fireworks, a parade, an arts and crafts fair, the Taste of Schaumburg and a variety of entertainment are all part of the 24th annual Schaumburg Septemberfest, held Saturday through Labor Day on the municipal grounds, 101 Schaumburg Ct.
"We want to provide something that keeps citizens in the community for the Labor Day weekend," says festival coordinator Chris Nelson.
Each year, the festival attendance has increased, last year drawing a crowd of between 250,000 and 300,000, according to Nelson.
For many residents, the festival highlight is the Labor Day parade. The mile-long parade will kick off at 10 a.m. and will follow Summit Drive, ending at the municipal grounds. The theme of the 90-unit parade is "That's Entertainment," and the parade features cars, floats and Miss Septemberfest and her court.
The Taste of Schaumburg promises to be a feast. The Taste will offer 24 food booths under a large tent with picnic tables and chairs, also on municipal grounds. Fare includes deep-dish pizza from Lou Malnati's, ribs from the Alumni Club, teriyaki chicken from Benihana Restaurant, and burgers from Fuddrucker's.
A carnival and game booths will satisfy younger crowds, while adults can enjoy perusing 250 different arts and crafts booths, featuring such items as hand-made wood carvings, Christmas decorations and dried flower arrangements. Some fine art vendors also will be on hand.
In the evening, the night air will be filled with music and the sky illuminated with fireworks on all three nights, including a special sky concert (fireworks put to music) Sept. 4.
The Septemberfest Committee has lined up some well-known bands for the main stage. The Saturday lineup includes Burnt Toast at 5 and 6:30 p.m., followed by Starship with Mickey Thomas at 8:30 p.m.
Joe Cantafio and the Giant Killers will kick off the entertainment on Sept. 4 at 5 and 6:30 p.m., followed by the Turtles at 8:30 p.m. And on Labor Day, Sept. 5, the stage will heat up with some country tunes performed by Little Country at 6 p.m. and the Bellamy Brothers at 8 p.m.
